---
import CaseStudy from "~/layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseHero from "~/components/case/CaseHero.astro";
import CaseLead from "~/components/case/CaseLead.astro";
import Closing from "~/components/case/Closing.astro";
import Gotchas from "~/components/case/Gotchas.astro";
import Hood from "~/components/case/Hood.astro";
import NextNav from "~/components/case/NextNav.astro";
import Scenes from "~/components/case/Scenes.astro";
import SceneStep from "~/components/case/SceneStep.astro";
import Section from "~/components/ui/Section.astro";
import cover from "~/assets/covers/platform-wide.webp";

const hood = [
  {
    icon: "aws-AmazonElasticKubernetesService",
    name: "EKS, and AKS on Azure",
    what: "Kubernetes, the same setup in every cluster",
  },
  { name: "Karpenter", what: "adds and removes nodes with the load" },
  {
    icon: "aws-ElasticLoadBalancingNetworkLoadBalancer",
    name: "Network Load Balancer + ingress",
    what: "the way into each cluster, with fixed IPs",
  },
  { name: "cert-manager", what: "certificates, so nobody renews one by hand" },
  {
    icon: "aws-AmazonAurora",
    name: "Aurora, Redis, OpenSearch",
    what: "ready-made modules a project picks from",
  },
  {
    icon: "aws-AmazonCloudFront",
    name: "CloudFront",
    what: "one entry, routing to Ireland or Tokyo by path",
  },
  {
    icon: "aws-AmazonSimpleStorageService",
    name: "Velero",
    what: "backups of the clusters, for the bad day",
  },
  { name: "Pulumi and Argo CD", what: "infrastructure and apps, both from git" },
  { name: "Rancher, Loki and Grafana", what: "one view of the whole fleet, and every pod’s logs" },
] as const;
const icons = hood.flatMap((h) => ("icon" in h ? [h.icon] : []));
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Hosting platform · Guus Ekkelenkamp"
  og="hosting-platform"
  description="Case study: the Kubernetes platform behind an agency's client projects, AWS first and the same on Azure."
  icons={icons}
>
  <CaseHero
    cover={cover}
    meta="Work at Harborn · 2022 to now · I designed and built it, and lead the team that runs it"
    title="Hosting platform"
    line="The AWS platform our client projects run on, and the same setup, adapted, on Azure where a client lives there."
  />
  <CaseLead>
    <p>
      When I started on this in 2022, every client project had its own setup. Its own pipelines, its own
      monitoring and its own way in, so each upgrade had to be worked out again, and so did each incident. I
      thought that could be one platform, so I started building it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Today it runs 8 Kubernetes clusters for Dutch and international clients, on AWS in Ireland and Tokyo and
      on Azure. The largest runs more than 1,500 pods. What makes it work is that a new client doesn’t get a
      new design. They get the same modules, the same network and the same way of deploying as everyone else,
      which also means one person can explain it to the next.
    </p>
  </CaseLead>
  <Scenes id="story" title="How the platform works">
    <SceneStep scene="standard">
      <h3>Many projects, one platform</h3>
      <p>
        Every client project used to sit on its own setup, and no two were quite the same. Now they all land
        on one platform: the same modules, the same network and the same way of deploying. A new client gets
        what everyone else already has.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="scale">
      <h3>It scales with the load</h3>
      <p>
        Traffic comes in through a load balancer and is spread over three availability zones. When the load
        goes up, Karpenter adds nodes and the pods spread onto them. When it calms down, the pods drain and
        the nodes are removed again. The largest cluster runs more than 1,500 pods this way.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="regions">
      <h3>Two regions, one way in</h3>
      <p>
        The platform runs in Ireland and in Tokyo. CloudFront sits in front of both: it sends region-specific
        paths to Tokyo and everything else to Europe, and static content comes straight from the edge.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="ipv6">
      <h3>The move to IPv6</h3>
      <p>
        Later we moved the clusters to IPv6-only. Anything that still only speaks IPv4 is reached through
        DNS64 and NAT64, which translate on the way out.
      </p>
      <p>
        It didn’t go without problems. A database answered on both an IPv4 and an IPv6 address, the apps
        picked IPv6, and those connections hung, because the security group only let IPv4 in. Opening it for
        IPv6 fixed it.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="learned">
      <h3>Everything as code</h3>
      <p>
        The infrastructure is Pulumi and the apps go through Argo CD, about 80 of them. Because every cluster
        comes from the same code, a change to the standard is made and reviewed once, not once per cluster.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some clients live on Azure. They get the same platform, adapted for AKS. That’s the part I’m proudest
        of: the standard holds on a second cloud.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
  </Scenes>
  <Section id="hood" title="Under the hood">
    <Hood items={[...hood]} />
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Infrastructure is Pulumi in Python, and we’re moving the older Terraform over gradually. Every change
        is a pull request: tests run against the modules, the preview runs with a mandatory CrossGuard policy
        pack and is saved as a plan, someone other than the author approves it, and the pipeline only applies
        what was approved. It runs on self-hosted runners with short-lived roles through GitHub OIDC, so CI
        holds no cloud keys.
      </p>
      <p>
        Apps go through git as well. Argo CD deploys acceptance from release candidates and production from
        stable release tags, and a repo that follows our conventions is onboarded by adding one GitHub topic.
        Every pod’s logs go to Loki.
      </p>
      <p>
        A small CronJob we built keeps chosen Deployments, ConfigMaps and Secrets in Tokyo in step with the
        main cluster, so a change is still made in one place. Every database, search and ingress version we
        offer has a published end-of-support date, so we can plan upgrades well before they’re due.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
  <Section id="lessons" title="Lessons" bodyClass="prose">
    <Gotchas
      items={[
        {
          symptom: "After the move to IPv6, apps hung when connecting to a database.",
          cause:
            "The database got a dual-stack address, so apps resolved both families, but the security group only allowed IPv4.",
          fix: "Allow IPv6 in the security group, and test the DNS64 and NAT64 path around the cluster as well.",
        },
        {
          symptom: "Queries over older logs failed or came back incomplete.",
          cause: "A lifecycle rule on the bucket deleted chunks that Loki’s index still pointed to.",
          fix: "Let Loki’s compactor own the 90-day retention and keep lifecycle rules off the log bucket.",
        },
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      The Tokyo syncer predates our move to GitOps. Today I’d replace it with Argo CD ApplicationSets that
      target both clusters, so git is the only source for both regions.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Closing line="I started this as one engineer who was tired of one-offs. What I took from it is that a standard only sticks when the people around you can run it without you, so these days I spend as much time teaching it as building it." />
  <NextNav
    prev={{ href: "/projects/", small: "Back to", label: "all projects" }}
    next={{ href: "/projects/homelab/", small: "Next project", label: "Homelab &rarr;" }}
  />
</CaseStudy>
